You are a high school art teacher, and you want to challenge your students to think creatively about the materials they have on hand. While they have access to a variety of traditional art supplies, you want them to explore the potential of a common piece of furniture found throughout the school: a chair. How can they use a chair in their art projects in innovative ways? Brainstorm and list as many unusual uses of a chair as you can think of.
Solution: 1. Convert the chair into a canvas by covering it with fabric or paper and having students paint or draw on it. 2. Disassemble the chair and use its parts as sculpture materials to create new forms and shapes. 3. Use the chair as a stand for displaying 3D art pieces or installations. 4. Attach various art tools (brushes, paints, markers) to the chair to create a mobile art supply station. 5. Transform the chair into a mosaic by gluing small pieces of colored paper, tile, or glass onto it. 6. Use the chair as a base for a papier-mâché project, building up layers to create sculptures or installations. 7. Hang the chair from the ceiling and use it as a mobile art structure, decorating it with hanging materials. 8. Reupholster the chair with unconventional materials like bubble wrap, foil, or carpet scraps to create a textured artwork. 9. Create a collage on the chair by attaching various found objects or images to its surface. 10. Use the chair as a frame for projecting art pieces, videos, or light displays. 11. Turn the chair into a shadow puppet theater, with students designing and performing puppet shows using the chair as the backdrop. 12. Incorporate the chair into a kinetic art piece, attaching moving parts or mechanisms to make it interactive. 13. Use the chair as a mold for creating casts or imprints with materials like clay or plaster. 14. Set up a light installation using the chair as the central point, with lights attached or positioned around it. 15. Disassemble the chair and reconfigure its parts to form a completely new, abstract structure.
